  "design": {
    "decision": "Count a guarded [Q] as a winner when bidding, then sequence the [A] and the [Q] of a suit (or the [Q] after partner's [A]) to run tricks. A [Q] can now be led into the opponents' [K].",
    "opponent": "Revealed the first time a [Q] takes an [A] or [K]. Opponents' only counter is to void the suit and trump.",
    "rationale": "This is card strength on a narrow set, in the family that measures well, but on a high rank, so the low-[♠] problem from the lesson does not arise. Only the [Q♠] becomes a top trump. ra-jq-become-aces is strong at rare. A [Q] alone is about half of it, and since this rule doesn't rename the card for payoffs it should land at uncommon. It shares a signature shape with uc-twos-beat, but that piece makes losers into winners for Low Cards and Nil. This one adds a second top card per suit for Streaks and Bid High, which is a different plan.",
    "partners": [
      "r2-four-row-x",
      "seed-four-row",
      "ub-low-to-aces",
      "ra-jq-become-aces",
      "r2-kings-untrumpable"
    ],
    "cluster": "R2",
    "draftText": "Your team's [Q]s beat every other card of their suit",
